Ladybird lays out a block container that has an automatic height and a
min-height once, and clamps its height afterward. It measures content
first only for other kinds of roots, such as grids, and form controls.

Blink does the same for block containers: Its BlockLayoutAlgorithm
lays out the children, and then ComputeBlockSizeForFragment() clamps
the block size. Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::ComputeFinalSize() clamps the
content block size with ReflowInput::ApplyMinMaxBSize() once reflow is
done. And WebKit’s RenderBlockFlow::layoutBlock() calls RenderBox::
updateLogicalHeight(), whose constrainLogicalHeightByMinMax() clamps
it. None of them lays a block container’s content out a second time
only to apply its min-height.

For a grid, Blink keeps the min-height inside GridLayoutAlgorithm, as
grid_min_available_size_ — which Ladybird’s measurement stands in for.
